Speed up OpenSBI's libfdt by trusting the (compiled, embedded) device tree.

OpenSBI's generic platform re-walks the FDT dozens of times during
fw_platform_init / fdt_serial_init, and every libfdt primitive
(fdt_offset_ptr, fdt_ro_probe_, ...) re-validates the header with byte-wise
big-endian reads. In RTL simulation of the NOEL-V core this dominates wall
time (~80% of executed instructions, hours to reach sbi_hart_pmp_configure).

Our FDT is produced by dtc and embedded in the firmware (fully trusted), so
default FDT_ASSUME_MASK to ASSUME_PERFECT (0xff): libfdt then skips the
redundant validation. Behavior is identical for a valid DTB; ~3-4x fewer
instructions through the FDT code.

--- a/lib/utils/libfdt/libfdt_internal.h
+++ b/lib/utils/libfdt/libfdt_internal.h
@@ -74,3 +74,3 @@
 #ifndef FDT_ASSUME_MASK
-#define FDT_ASSUME_MASK 0
+#define FDT_ASSUME_MASK 0xff
 #endif
